Plugin I/O, part two: what the Tailscale, Home Assistant and phone plugins
still lacked.

- net Socket.write takes a Uint8Array as well as a string. The string-or-bytes
  argument is one helper, host::bytes_arg, shared with child.write.
- fs/promises open(path, "r" | "w" | "a" | "r+") returns a FileHandle with
  read(maxBytes?), write(data) and close(), each at most 1 MiB per call and
  run off the main thread. "r" needs a read grant, "w"/"a" a write grant,
  "r+" both. An anchor task closes the file when the view or plugin goes away.
- fetch(url, { unix }) sends HTTP/1.1 over a unix socket granted in
  Capabilities::network_unix, via hyper on a current-thread tokio runtime
  (both already in the tree through reqwest). The URL host only fills Host;
  path and query are the target. Buffered and stream: true responses and the
  timeout work as over TCP; redirects are not followed. Origin is now a
  refused request header everywhere.
- ChildProcess.closeStdin() closes stdin after the queued writes. When the
  owner goes away the child's group gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 2 s later.

